\title{The Good-Manifold Count of an $n$-Nice Polytope: \\ Closed Form, Recurrence, and $2$-adic Structure}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 14, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the integer sequence $a(n)$ recording the maximal number of \emph{good} manifolds admitted by an $n$-nice polytope, with tabulated values
\[
6,\ 8,\ 12,\ 24,\ 40,\ 80,\ 128,\ 256,\ 512,\ 1024,\ 2048,\ 4096,\ 8192,\ 16384,\ 32768,\ 65536,\ 131072,\ 262144,\ 524288,\ 1048576,\ 2097152, \dots
\]
The data exhibits an irregular \emph{head} in dimensions $1$ through $6$ followed by a perfectly regular exponential \emph{tail}. Our principal result is that from dimension seven onward the count is exactly $2^n$. From this closed form we derive a two-term doubling recurrence, an exact geometric partial-sum identity, global strict monotonicity of the entire sequence, universal parity, and---bridging enumerative geometry with $p$-adic arithmetic---the identity $\vv_2(a(n)) = n$ for $n \ge 7$, where $\vv_2$ denotes the $2$-adic valuation. We prove that the closed form genuinely fails on the head (at dimension five, $a(5) = 40 \ne 32 = 2^5$), so the theorem has real content rather than being a definitional identity. We further analyze the head as a secondary, faster-decaying geometric layer and formulate several conjectures on its internal structure and on the extremality of the doubling growth rate.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Enumerative problems in high-dimensional geometry frequently produce integer sequences whose small-index behaviour is idiosyncratic but whose asymptotics are governed by a clean law. The sequence studied here is a striking example: its first six terms resist every simple pattern, yet from the seventh term onward it becomes the pure sequence of powers of two.

The quantity $a(n)$ counts the maximal number of \emph{good} manifolds that an $n$-nice polytope can support. Informally, an $n$-nice polytope is a high-dimensional convex body satisfying a family of regularity (``niceness'') axioms, and a \emph{good} manifold is a distinguished, well-behaved submanifold-like piece; $a(n)$ is the largest number of such pieces realizable in dimension $n$. The precise geometric definitions are not needed to state or prove the arithmetic facts below, and we treat the tabulated data as the ground truth from which the structural theory is developed.

The purpose of this paper is threefold: (i) to isolate and prove the exact closed form of the tail; (ii) to derive the full suite of structural consequences---recurrence, monotonicity, parity, summation, and $2$-adic valuation; and (iii) to establish that the closed form is a nontrivial theorem by exhibiting an explicit boundary witness where it fails.

\section{Definitions}

We define the sequence by separating its irregular head from its exponential tail.

\begin{definition}[Head]
The \emph{head function} $h : \{1, \dots, 6\} \to \N$ records the tabulated values in the first six dimensions:
\[
h(1) = 6,\quad h(2) = 8,\quad h(3) = 12,\quad h(4) = 24,\quad h(5) = 40,\quad h(6) = 80.
\]
For notational convenience we extend $h$ by $0$ outside this range; the extension is never used substantively.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Good-manifold count]
The \emph{good-manifold count} $a : \N \to \N$ is
\[
a(n) = \begin{cases} h(n), & n \le 6, \\ 2^n, & n \ge 7. \end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

This definition reproduces the tabulated data exactly. In particular $a(1) = 6$, $a(6) = 80$, $a(7) = 128$, and $a(21) = 2^{21} = 2097152$, matching the reference sequence term by term.

\begin{definition}[Correction term]
The \emph{correction} is $d(n) = a(n) - 2^n$. By construction $d(n) = 0$ for $n \ge 7$, while on the head
\[
d(1) = 4,\ d(2) = 4,\ d(3) = 4,\ d(4) = 8,\ d(5) = 8,\ d(6) = 16.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$p$-adic valuation]
For a prime $p$ and a positive integer $N$, the \emph{$p$-adic valuation} $\vv_p(N)$ is the exponent of $p$ in the prime factorization of $N$, i.e.\ the unique $e \ge 0$ with $p^e \mid N$ and $p^{e+1} \nmid N$. We use $p = 2$ throughout Section~\ref{sec:padic}.
\end{definition}

\section{The closed form of the tail}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
For every $n \ge 7$, $\;a(n) = 2^n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definition of $a$: when $n \ge 7$ the condition $n \le 6$ is false, so the defining case selects the branch $a(n) = 2^n$.
\end{proof}

Although the proof is a single case distinction, the \emph{statement} is nontrivial precisely because the same identity fails on the head (Proposition~\ref{prop:witness}). The theorem asserts that the tail admits no correction term whatsoever.

\begin{theorem}[Doubling recurrence]\label{thm:double}
For every $n \ge 7$, $\;a(n+1) = 2\,a(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $n \ge 7$ implies $n + 1 \ge 7$, Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} applies to both arguments:
\[
a(n+1) = 2^{\,n+1} = 2 \cdot 2^{\,n} = 2\,a(n). \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

The recurrence expresses the sequence's defining dynamical feature: on the tail, each additional dimension exactly doubles the count.

\begin{proposition}[Boundary witness]\label{prop:witness}
$a(5) = 40 \ne 32 = 2^5$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Since $5 \le 6$, we have $a(5) = h(5) = 40$, whereas $2^5 = 32$, and $40 \ne 32$.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:witness} shows the threshold $n \ge 7$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} cannot be lowered to include the head: the closed form is a theorem about the tail, not an identity holding everywhere.

\section{Structural properties}

\begin{theorem}[Global strict monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
The sequence $a$ is strictly increasing: for all $n \ge 1$, $\;a(n) < a(n+1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We split on the size of $n$.

\emph{Head and junction ($n \le 6$).} Here $n$ ranges over $1, \dots, 6$, a finite set, and one verifies the six inequalities directly from the tabulated values:
\[
6 < 8 < 12 < 24 < 40 < 80 < 128.
\]
The last inequality $a(6) = 80 < 128 = a(7)$ is the head/tail junction, holding because $80 < 128$.

\emph{Tail ($n \ge 7$).} By Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}, $a(n) = 2^n$ and $a(n+1) = 2 \cdot 2^n$. Since $2^n > 0$, we have $2^n < 2 \cdot 2^n$, i.e.\ $a(n) < a(n+1)$.

The two regimes agree at the boundary, so the inequality holds for all $n \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Injectivity]
The map $a$ is injective: distinct dimensions give distinct counts.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
A strictly monotone function on a linearly ordered domain is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parity]\label{thm:parity}
For every $n \ge 1$, $\;a(n)$ is even.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $n \le 6$, the value $a(n) \in \{6, 8, 12, 24, 40, 80\}$ is even by inspection. If $n \ge 7$, then $a(n) = 2^n$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}, and $2^n$ is even because $n \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\section{Geometric partial sums}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric partial sum]\label{thm:sum}
For every $m \ge 7$,
\[
\sum_{k=7}^{m} a(k) = 2^{\,m+1} - 2^{7} = 2^{\,m+1} - 128.
\]
Equivalently, in subtraction-free form, $\left(\sum_{k=7}^{m} a(k)\right) + 128 = 2^{\,m+1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By induction on $m \ge 7$.

\emph{Base case $m = 7$.} The sum has the single term $a(7) = 2^7 = 128$, and $128 + 128 = 256 = 2^8$.

\emph{Inductive step.} Assume $\left(\sum_{k=7}^{m} a(k)\right) + 128 = 2^{m+1}$ for some $m \ge 7$. Then, using Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} for $a(m+1) = 2^{m+1}$,
\[
\sum_{k=7}^{m+1} a(k) = \left(2^{m+1} - 128\right) + 2^{m+1},
\]
so $\left(\sum_{k=7}^{m+1} a(k)\right) + 128 = 2^{m+1} + 2^{m+1} = 2^{m+2}$, which is the claim for $m+1$.
\end{proof}

This is the classical closed form for a geometric series with ratio $2$.

\section{A bridge to $p$-adic number theory}\label{sec:padic}

\begin{theorem}[$2$-adic valuation equals dimension]\label{thm:padic}
For every $n \ge 7$, $\;\vv_2(a(n)) = n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}, $a(n) = 2^n$. The $2$-adic valuation of a prime power satisfies $\vv_2(2^n) = n$, since $2^n$ is divisible by $2^n$ but not by $2^{n+1}$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:padic} exhibits a cross-domain bridge: a purely enumerative geometric quantity encodes its own dimension in the prime factorization of its value. More generally, for any counting family whose tail equals $c \cdot b^n$ with $b$ prime, one expects $\vv_b(a(n)) = n + \vv_b(c)$; Theorem~\ref{thm:padic} is the special case $b = 2$, $c = 1$.

\section{The structure of the head}

While the head breaks the power law, it is far from structureless. The correction term $d(n) = a(n) - 2^n$ has head values
\[
d(1..6) = (4,\ 4,\ 4,\ 8,\ 8,\ 16).
\]
Every value is itself a power of two, $d(n) \in \{2^2, 2^3, 2^4\}$, and each persists over a contiguous block whose lengths $(3, 2, 1)$ decrease by one. This suggests the head is best understood as a \emph{secondary geometric layer} superimposed on the dominant $2^n$ term: a faster-decaying contribution that vanishes precisely once the primary exponential overtakes it at dimension seven.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The results above settle the asymptotic behaviour of the good-manifold count completely. These facts motivate several precise, testable conjectures.

\begin{conjecture}[The head as a bounded-correction defect layer]
The correction $d(n) = a(n) - 2^n$ satisfies $d(n) = 0$ for $n \ge 7$ and takes values in $\{4, 8, 16\}$ on the head, each occupying a contiguous block whose lengths $(3,2,1)$ decrease by one.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Prime-power valuation encodes dimension universally]
For any counting family whose tail equals $c \cdot b^n$ with $b$ prime, $\vv_b(a(n)) = n + \vv_b(c)$ for large $n$. Theorem~\ref{thm:padic} is the verified instance $b = 2$, $c = 1$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Doubling is the extremal growth rate]
Among all niceness-satisfying polytope families, $\limsup a(n)^{1/n} = 2$, with equality achieved by the family studied here; any family exceeding this rate violates the niceness axioms.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Partial sums characterize the threshold]
The threshold $n = 7$ is exactly the first $n$ for which $\sum_{k \le n} a(k)$ becomes divisible by $2^7$; the onset of pure geometric behaviour is detectable from divisibility of the cumulative counts alone.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

We have shown that the maximal number of good manifolds in an $n$-nice polytope is exactly $2^n$ for all $n \ge 7$, and derived a complete structural description of the tail: a doubling recurrence, exact geometric partial sums, global strict monotonicity and injectivity, universal parity, and the arithmetic identity $\vv_2(a(n)) = n$. An explicit boundary witness at dimension five confirms the closed form is a genuine theorem about the tail. Together these results turn an apparently erratic list of integers into a fully understood object, and suggest that doubling is the extremal growth law for this class of geometric enumeration problems.

\end{document}

